A||N||4||glava2876||84 brake line leak||||5||
Z||000000||glava2876||06-08-2001||08:34 PM||||Well I finished installing my clean gas tank, installed a TBI new gasket and "O" ring set and the Duke is alive after sleeping for 10+ years. Next problem is a brake leak. With the car still on stands I was running it through the gears and loading it a little by applying the brakes. All of a sudden I lost most of the brakes. I looked under the car and on the rear passenger side there was a fluid puddle. I will dig into this over the weekend, but it looks to be leaking at the block where the single line splits into two to go to each rear brake (The brake hose on this side and the other  looks fine). Will this be a simple matter of replacing one of the three lines or will there be something else? Is this a common problem? Thanks, Glen||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||06-09-2001||12:17 AM||||Welcome to the Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>See if the lines are leaking further up. I know someone was mentioning the rubber hoses can split from cantamination and age. You could have someone push the pedal while you look around under there. Let us know what you find.||||reg||1
Z||000002||kslish||06-09-2001||02:02 AM||||The steel lines can also eventually get pinhole leaks from rust (just had my steel lines replaced on my coupe because of this).<P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||SteveJ||06-09-2001||08:31 AM||||Yup, I just did this about a month ago. Had a leak just behind that junction block. When I tried to unscrew that line it was frozen. Unscrewing it wiggled the front line just enough to fracture it too as it was also rusted. Wound up replacing all the line from the master cylinder back. The passenger rear actually came off ok but by the time you have the system apart you might as well do that too as its only eight inches or so more line. In order to get the nuts to unscrew from the block we had to take it out of the car and wrestle with it. <BR>When we finished with that we did the fronts because we were dirty and figured they were the same age as the rears.<BR>A tubing bender is an absolute necessity. The hardest part is the bends near the master cylinder and going through the floor.<BR>We used the tubing from Autozone because it has a green plastic coating on it to prevent rust.<BR>Make sure your bleeder valves are loose. Dose 'em with WD40 or rust penatrating oil a coulple of times in the day or so before you do the job or you will wind up bleeding from the nut where the hose attaches to the caliper which isn't so good.<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 06-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||glava2876||06-09-2001||09:22 AM||||Thanks for the responses. I'll clean up the area to veify where the brake fluid is leaking and then hit the line connections and bleeders with WD-40.I don't think I'll replace all of the lines unless they look real bad. I have a 79 Z/28 with 29,000 miles that still has the original lines -I just flush the fluid every 2-3 years. Thanks, Glen  ||||reg||10
